# Folder shapes design

Which files and folders a project folder may hold, and what Fix changes in a project folder.
A project folder is the folder of a registered project of any kind (see ControllersDesign).

The algorithms of this design are a contract on the behaviour: a specification, not a suggested implementation.
They may be very slow or otherwise inefficient. They are designed to be obviously correct; implementing them as they are may be a terrible idea.

## Desks

A D models a 'desk': an operating system with the file systems it mounts.
```
D ::= ids; folders
folder ::= id{Es}
E ::= s -> e
e ::= id | file | other
p ::= id s1..sn
z ::= {Zs}
Z ::= s -> z | s -> file
t ::= e | z
v ::= visible | hidden
```
- In a D, the ids of the folders are distinct.
- In a D, every id of the ids, and every id of an E of the folders, is the id of a folder of the folders.
- In a folder, the s of the Es are distinct.
- In a z, the s of the Zs are distinct.

`s`, `file` and `other` are atoms; a `file` has a content and a time. `len(s)` is a natural number.
`isHidden(s)`, `isZip(s)`, `isZipOf(s1, s2)`, `folderNameOk(s)`, `fileNameOk(s)`, `hiddenNameOk(s)`, `okTogether(s1, s2)` and `timeOk(file)` are predicates, and `zip(file)` is a z or undefined.
The implementation notes define them for each desk.
The ids of a D are the 'root ids'.
```
at(D, id)= id                 with D= _ id _; _
at(D, p s)= e                 with at(D, p)= id, id{_ s -> e _} in D
at(D, p s)= t                 with at(D, p)= file, zip(file)= {_ s -> t _}
at(D, p s)= t                 with at(D, p)= {_ s -> t _}
root(D, id s1..sn)            iff  n= 0, D= _ id _; _
add(D, p s, e)= ids; folders id{Es s -> e} folders'
                     with D= ids; folders id{Es} folders'
                          at(D, p)= id
                          at(D, p s) undefined
create(D, p s, file)= add(D, p s, file)
makeFolder(D, p s)= add(ids; folders id{}, p s, id)
                     with D= ids; folders
                          id is not the id of a folder of D
move(D, p1 s1, p2 s2)= add(delete(D, p1 s1), p2 s2, at(D, p1 s1))
delete(D, p s)= ids; folders id{Es Es'} folders'
                     with D= ids; folders id{Es s -> _ Es'} folders'
                          at(D, p)= id
rename(D, p s, s')= ids; folders id{Es s' -> e Es'} folders'
                     with D= ids; folders id{Es s -> e Es'} folders'
                          at(D, p)= id
                          at(D, p s') undefined
write(D, p s, file')= ids; folders id{Es s -> file' Es'} folders'
                     with D= ids; folders id{Es s -> file Es'} folders'
                          at(D, p)= id
```
When `at(D, p)` is a file f, or a z inside zip(f), `add`, `delete`, `rename` and `write` on `p s` change zip(f), and f is written again from the changed zip(f), with the current time.
A `move` of a z to a folder of D adds a folder with a new id, holding the Zs of that z, each z among them moved in the same way.
A 'checkable folder property' is a predicate on Ds and ps that never compares two ids; `valid`, `same` and `inside` below are 'checkable folder properties'.
```
names(D, id)= Es              with id{Es} in D
names(D, {Zs})= Zs
names(D, file)= names(D, zip(file))
valid(D, p)         iff  at(D, p)= id, names(D, id) not empty, ok(D, names(D, id), visible, 0, 0)
ok(D, s1 -> t1 .. sk -> tk, v, l, d)
                    iff  forall i, j in 1..k with i != j: okTogether(si, sj)
                         forall i, j in 1..k with i != j: (v= visible, not isHidden(si), isZip(si)) implies (not isZipOf(si, sj))
                         forall i in 1..k: okEntry(D, si, ti, v, l+len(si), d)
okEntry(D, s, t, v, L, d)
                    iff  one of these holds:
                         v= visible, not isHidden(s), L <= 200, (t= id or t= z), folderNameOk(s),
                           names(D, t) not empty, ok(D, names(D, t), visible, L+1, d)
                         v= visible, not isHidden(s), L <= 200, t= file, fileNameOk(s), timeOk(t), not isZip(s)
                         v= visible, not isHidden(s), L <= 200, t= file, fileNameOk(s), timeOk(t), isZip(s), d <= 64,
                           zip(t)= z, names(D, z) not empty, ok(D, names(D, z), visible, L-3, d+1)
                         (v= hidden or isHidden(s)), L <= 250, t= file, hiddenNameOk(s)
                         (v= hidden or isHidden(s)), L <= 250, (t= id or t= z), hiddenNameOk(s),
                           (v= visible implies names(D, t) not empty), ok(D, names(D, t), hidden, L+1, d)
same(D, p1, p2)     iff  at(D, p1)= id1, at(D, p2)= id2,
                         exists s, file: at(D, p1 s) undefined, at(D, p2 s) undefined, at(create(D, p1 s, file), p2 s)= file
inside(D, p1, p2)   iff  at(D, p1)= id1, at(D, p2)= id2, exists s1..sn with n > 0: same(D, p1, p2 s1..sn)
```

## Entries

An 'entry' is a p s1..sn, with p the project folder, n > 0 and `at(D, p s1..sn)` defined, such that each file that `at` goes through is a 'zip'.
An 'entry' p s1..sn is 'hidden' when `isHidden(si)` for an i in 1..n, and 'visible' otherwise.
A 'zip' is a 'visible' 'entry' p s1..sn with `at(D, p s1..sn)` a file and `isZip(sn)`.
The 'path' of an 'entry' p s1..sn is s1, ..., sn joined with `/`, where each si, with i < n, of a 'zip' p s1..si counts without its ending `.zip`: the file `a.fear` of `lib/x.zip` has the 'path' `lib/x/a.fear`.

## Shape rules

A project folder p is 'well shaped' when `valid(D, p)` holds; the 'shape rules' are the premises of `valid`, `ok` and `okEntry`.
The 'problem' (see Project states in ControllersDesign) of a project whose project folder is not 'well shaped' states one broken 'shape rule' and lists the 'paths' breaking that 'shape rule' (none for a project folder holding no 'entry').
The code checking the 'shape rules' is `Coordinator/src/realSourceOracle`.

## Names

A 'valid segment' holds only lowercase letters (`a` to `z`), digits (`0` to `9`) and `_`, starts with a letter or `_`, and is not `con`, `prn`, `aux`, `nul`, `com1` to `com9` or `lpt1` to `lpt9`.
A 'valid file name' is one of:
- a 'valid segment', a dot and an extension of 1 to 16 lowercase letters or digits (`0` to `9`);
- a 'valid segment', a dot and one of `css.map`, `d.ts`, `d.ts.map`, `js.map`, `min.css`, `min.js`, `tar.bz2`, `tar.gz`, `tar.lz`, `tar.lz4`, `tar.xz`, `tar.zst`.

A 'hidden name':
- does not end with a dot or a space;
- holds no control character, no U+FFFD, no surrogate without its pair, and none of `<`, `>`, `:`, `"`, `/`, `\`, `|`, `?`, `*`;
- before the first dot, without its ending spaces, in lowercase, is not `con`, `prn`, `aux`, `nul`, `conin$`, `conout$`, `com1` to `com9`, `lpt1` to `lpt9`, or `com` or `lpt` followed by U+00B9, U+00B2 or U+00B3;
- is at most 255 bytes long in UTF-8.

A 'zip file' is a file in the zip format where each file its central directory stores is stored or deflated, and not encrypted.
The 'stored names' of a 'zip file' are the names its central directory stores. The 'stored names' are 'good' when:
- every name is valid UTF-8, holds no NUL character, and, without one `/` at its end, holds no segment that is empty, `.` or `..`;
- no name is stored twice, comparing the names without one `/` at their end;
- no name starts with the name of a file followed by `/`.

## Fix

Fix is an operation of the manager on one project (`Fix <name>` in ControllersDesign).

A 'junk file' is one of:
- a file named `.DS_Store`;
- a file whose name starts with `._`;
- a file named `Icon` followed by a carriage return;
- a file named `Thumbs.db`, `ehthumbs.db`, `ehthumbs_vista.db` or `desktop.ini`, with each letter `a` to `z` in uppercase or in lowercase;
- a file whose name ends with `:Zone.Identifier`;
- a file named `.directory`;
- a file whose name ends with `~`;
- a file whose name starts with `~$`;
- a file whose name starts with `.~lock.` and ends with `#`;
- a file whose name starts with `#` and ends with `#`;
- a file whose name starts with `.` and ends with `.swp`.
A 'junk folder' is a folder named `__MACOSX` or `.AppleDouble`, or a folder whose name starts with `.Trash-`.

The 'fixed segment' of a text is that text with `A` to `Z` turned into `a` to `z`, with every sequence of characters other than `a` to `z`, `0` to `9` and `_` turned into one `_`, and with a leading `p` when the result is not a 'valid segment'.
The 'guessed extension' of a file is:
- `png` when the content of the file starts with the bytes `89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A`;
- `jpg` when the content of the file starts with the bytes `FF D8 FF`;
- `gif` when the content of the file starts with the text `GIF87a` or `GIF89a`;
- `txt` when the content of the file is UTF-8 text holding no control character other than tab, line feed and carriage return; a file with no content is such a file;
- `bin` otherwise.
The 'fixed name' of an 'entry' p s with `at(D, p s)` an id or a z is the 'fixed segment' of s.
The 'fixed name' of an 'entry' p s with `at(D, p s)` a file is the 'fixed segment' of the stem of s, a dot and the extension of s, where the stem is s without the dot and the extension, and the extension is:
- `css.map`, `d.ts`, `d.ts.map`, `js.map`, `min.css`, `min.js`, `tar.bz2`, `tar.gz`, `tar.lz`, `tar.lz4`, `tar.xz` or `tar.zst`, when the name in lowercase ends with a dot and that extension;
- otherwise the end of the name after the last dot, in lowercase, when that end is 1 to 16 letters (`a` to `z`, `A` to `Z`) or digits (`0` to `9`);
- otherwise the 'guessed extension' of the file; the stem is then the whole name.
The 'fixed name' number k of an 'entry' is its 'fixed name' with k added at the end of the 'fixed segment'.
Two names 'clash' when the two names are equal after removing an ending `.zip` in any letter case, Unicode normalization (NFC) and Unicode simple case folding.

The 'cleaned file' of a file is the file, with the current time, whose content is the result of the run of `StrCleaners.softText` (see TextCleanersDesign) on the UTF-8 bytes of the text of the file, after turning U+2018 and U+2019 into `'`, U+201C and U+201D into `"`, and U+2013, U+2014 and U+2212 into `-`.
The text of the file is the UTF-16 text when the file starts with a UTF-16 byte order mark (the bytes `FF FE` or `FE FF`) and the bytes after it are valid UTF-16, the UTF-8 text when the bytes of the file are valid UTF-8, and the windows-1252 text otherwise, where the bytes `EF BB BF` at the start of the file are a byte order mark and each byte that windows-1252 does not define is U+FFFD.
The 'cleaning lines' of a file are `Removed the byte order mark of <path>`, `Fixed the line endings of <path>` and `Cleaned <k> characters of <path>` (`Cleaned 1 character of <path>` when `<k>` is 1): the first when the text starts with a byte order mark, the second when the line endings change, the third when `<k>` is more than 0; `<k>` is the number of code points of the text, after the byte order mark is removed and the line endings are fixed, other than the line feed and U+0020 to U+007E.

The 'restored names' of a 'zip file' are its 'stored names' where every `\` becomes `/`, and each name is then its segments that are neither empty nor `.`, joined with `/`, with a `/` at the end when the stored name ends with `/` or `\`; a name ending with `/` or `\` with no such segment is no longer stored, and any other name with no such segment is the empty name.
A 'zip file' written from a z keeps, for each file that Fix does not change, its name, its content, the time the 'zip file' stores for it and its place in the central directory of the 'zip file'.
A 'zip file' written from a z stores only names, contents and times: not the comment of the 'zip file', and no other data stored for a file, like its permissions.

In the pseudocode, `say <line>` adds a line at the end of the Output of the project, and `end <line>` adds a line and ends Fix.
`<path>` is the 'path' of the 'entry' `p s` in the D before the operation of the same line.
The 'fix folder' of a Fix on a project folder named `<f>` is the first of `<f>_fix`, `<f>_fix2`, `<f>_fix3`, ... that 'clashes' with no name in the folder holding the project folder when the Fix starts.
```
fix(p)
  empty the Output
  fixFolder(p, visible, 0, 0)
  if the Output is empty: say `The project "<name>" needs no fix.`, with <name> the name of the project

fixFolder(p, v, l, d)
  for each s of an s -> t of names(D, at(D, p)) when fixFolder starts, in the order of the s: fixEntry(p, s, v, l, d)

fixEntry(p, s, v, l, d)
  t= at(D, p s)
  v'= hidden if v= hidden or isHidden(s), visible otherwise
  if (t= file and a file named s is a 'junk file') or ((t= id or t= z) and a folder named s is a 'junk folder'):
    D= delete(D, p s); say `Deleted <path>`; return
  if t= other: away(p s); return
  if v'= visible and (((t= id or t= z) and not folderNameOk(s)) or (t= file and not fileNameOk(s))
                      or (isZip(s) and isZipOf(s, s'') for an s'' of an s -> t of names(D, at(D, p)))):
    s'= the first of the 'fixed name' of p s, its 'fixed name' number 2, number 3, ...,
        with folderNameOk(s') when t= id or t= z, with fileNameOk(s') and a file named s' not a 'junk file' when t= file, clashing with no s of another s -> t of names(D, at(D, p))
    D= rename(D, p s, s'); say `Renamed <path> to <s'>`; s= s'
  L= l+len(s)
  if (v'= hidden and not hiddenNameOk(s)) or (v'= visible and L > 200) or L > 250
     or not okTogether(s'', s) for an s'' that this fixFolder has already handled and that is the s of an s -> t of names(D, at(D, p)):
    away(p s); return
  if v'= visible and t= file and s ends with `.fear` and the content of the 'cleaned file' of t is not the content of t:
    D= write(D, p s, the 'cleaned file' of t); say each of the 'cleaning lines' of t
  t= at(D, p s)
  if t= file and not timeOk(t):
    D= write(D, p s, t with the current time); say `Reset the time of <path>`
  if t= id or t= z:
    fixFolder(p s, v', L+1, d)
    if v= visible and names(D, at(D, p s)) is empty: D= delete(D, p s); say `Deleted the empty folder <path>`
  if t= file and v'= visible and isZip(s):
    fixZip(p s, L-3, d+1)

fixZip(p s, l, d)
  t= at(D, p s), with the 'restored names' in place of its 'stored names'
  if zip(t) is undefined or d > 65: away(p s); return
  if the 'restored names' differ from the 'stored names': D= write(D, p s, t with the current time); say `Fixed the stored names of <path>`
  fixFolder(p s, visible, l, d)
  if zip(at(D, p s)) holds no Z: D= delete(D, p s); say `Deleted the empty zip <path>`

away(p s)
  q= the 'fix folder'
  with s1, ..., sn the segments of the 'path' of p s: if at(D, q s1..si) is a file or an other for an i in 1..n: end `file already exists:` and the 'disk path' of q s1..si
  D= makeFolder of q, when q is missing, and of each folder of the 'path' of p s below q that is missing
  D= move(D, p s, the 'path' of p s below q)
  say `Moved <path> to <the 'disk path' of the destination>`
```
When Fix does not `end`, `valid(D, p)` holds after Fix, unless the project folder holds no 'entry'.
A `delete`, a `rename`, a `write`, a `makeFolder` or a `move` that the desk refuses ends Fix with `end` and the error of the desk and the 'disk path' that the desk refuses; for a `rename` refused because the desk takes s' as the name of another 'entry', that error is `file already exists:`.

## Implementation notes

The sections below say, for each desk, which operations of the desk correspond to which operations on a D.
An operation of a desk is a program run in a shell: `sh` on Linux and macOS, PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`) on Windows.
Each correspondence holds while no other program changes the folders that the operation reads or writes.
A 'disk path' is a path as the desk writes it; `<disk path>`, `<disk path1>` and `<disk path2>` are the 'disk paths' corresponding to p, p1 and p2.

An id corresponds to a folder of the desk.
A mount point corresponds to a 'root id'.
The pair of the device number and the inode number that a desk gives for a folder does not correspond to an id.
Two mount points of one shared folder of the network can give that folder two different device numbers, so two different pairs, while `same` holds for the two ps.

### Linux

- An s corresponds to 1 or more bytes, none of them `0x00` or `0x2F` (`/`), other than `.` and `..`.
- `len(s)` is the number of bytes of s.
- `isHidden(s)`: the first byte of s is `0x2E` (`.`).
- `isZip(s)`: the last four bytes of s are the ASCII bytes of `.zip`.
- `isZipOf(s1, s2)`: s1 is the bytes of s2 followed by the ASCII bytes of `.zip`.
- `folderNameOk(s)`: s is the ASCII bytes of a 'valid segment' (see Names).
- `fileNameOk(s)`: s is the ASCII bytes of a 'valid file name' (see Names).
- `hiddenNameOk(s)`: s is valid UTF-8, and the text of s is a 'hidden name' (see Names).
- `okTogether(s1, s2)`: s1 and s2 are not both valid UTF-8 with texts equal after Unicode normalization (NFC) and Unicode simple case folding.
- `timeOk(file)`: the time of the file is after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z and at most 5 seconds after the current time. The time of a file of the folders is the last modified time, the number of seconds that the program `stat`, run as `stat -c %Y <disk path>`, prints; the time of a file of a z is the time the 'zip file' stores for that file.
- `zip(file)`: the tree of the 'stored names' of the 'zip file' (see Names), split at `/`, each segment as its UTF-8 bytes, where a name ending with `/` is a z; undefined when the file is not a 'zip file', or its 'stored names' are not 'good'.
- The mount points are the paths in the fifth field of each line of the file `/proc/self/mountinfo`, with each escape `\ooo` (three octal digits) read as the byte it stands for, for which `stat -c %F` prints `directory`, leaving out a line when another line, not on the chain of parents of that line (the second field of a line is the first field of the line of its parent), has a mount point that the path of that line starts with, comparing whole names. `/` is one of them.
- A 'disk path' given to a program, whose names after the mount point `m` are s1, ..., sn, corresponds to the p `id s1..sn`, where `m` is the longest mount point the path starts with, comparing whole names, and `id` is the 'root id' corresponding to `m`.
  This holds when each `si` is one of the names that the program `find` prints for the path of `m` and s1, ..., si-1, as below, and `stat` prints `directory` for each of the paths ending at s1, ..., sn-1.
  For example, with the mount points `/` and `/home`, the path `/home/user/proj` corresponds to `id user proj`, with `id` corresponding to `/home`.
- The program `find`, run as `find <disk path> -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -printf '%f\0'`, prints the s of the Es of the folder `at(D, p)`, each followed by the byte `0x00`.
- The program `stat`, run as `stat -c %F <disk path>`:
  - prints `directory` when `at(D, p)` is an id;
  - prints `regular file` or `regular empty file` when `at(D, p)` is a file;
  - prints `symbolic link`, `fifo`, `socket`, `character special file`, `block special file`, or any other text, when `at(D, p)` is an other;
  - fails with `No such file or directory` when `at(D, p)` is undefined.
- The shell command `[ ! -e <disk path>/s ] && [ ! -L <disk path>/s ] && : > <disk path>/s` creates the file `<disk path>/s`, and fails when `at(D, p s)` is defined; it corresponds to `create(D, p s, file)` for the file with no content and the current time.
- The program `rm`, run as `rm -r <disk path>/s`, corresponds to `delete(D, p s)` when no mount point m has `same(D, m, p s)` or `inside(D, m, p s)`; otherwise the desk refuses `delete(D, p s)`.
- The program `mkdir`, run as `mkdir <disk path>/s`, corresponds to `makeFolder(D, p s)`.
- The shell command `[ ! -L <disk path2>/s2 ] && [ ! -e <disk path2>/s2 ] && mv -T <disk path1>/s1 <disk path2>/s2` corresponds to `move(D, p1 s1, p2 s2)` when no mount point m has `same(D, m, p1 s1)` or `inside(D, m, p1 s1)`; otherwise the desk refuses `move(D, p1 s1, p2 s2)`.
- The shell command `[ ! -L <disk path>/s' ] && { [ ! -e <disk path>/s' ] || [ <disk path>/s -ef <disk path>/s' ]; } && mv -T <disk path>/s <disk path>/<n> && mv -T <disk path>/<n> <disk path>/s'`, with `<n>` an s for which `[ ! -e <disk path>/<n> ] && [ ! -L <disk path>/<n> ]` holds, corresponds to `rename(D, p s, s')`.
- Writing the bytes of `file'` in `<disk path>/s`, then running the program `touch` as `touch -m -d @<seconds> <disk path>/s` with the time of `file'`, corresponds to `write(D, p s, file')` when `stat -c %h <disk path>/s` prints `1` and no mount point is `<disk path>/s`; otherwise the desk refuses `write(D, p s, file')`.
- The order of the s: the first byte that differs decides, and a shorter s comes before the longer s it starts.
- The 'junk file', 'junk folder', 'fixed segment', 'fixed name' and 'clash' rules read s as text: the UTF-8 text of s, where each byte that is not part of valid UTF-8 is a character other than `a` to `z`, digits and `_`.

### macOS

- An s corresponds to bytes, as on Linux.
- `len(s)` is the number of bytes of s, as on Linux.
- `isHidden(s)`, `isZip(s)`, `isZipOf(s1, s2)`, `folderNameOk(s)`, `fileNameOk(s)`, `hiddenNameOk(s)`, `okTogether(s1, s2)` and `zip(file)`: as on Linux.
- `timeOk(file)`: as on Linux, with the program `stat` run as `stat -f %m <disk path>`.
- The mount points are the paths that the program `mount`, run with no argument, prints: one line for each mount point, with the path between the first ` on ` and the last ` (`. `/` is one of them.
- A 'disk path' given to a program corresponds to a p as on Linux.
- The program `find`, run as `find <disk path> -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -print0`, prints `<disk path>/` followed by each s of the Es of the folder `at(D, p)`, each followed by the byte `0x00`.
- The program `stat`, run as `stat -f %HT <disk path>`:
  - prints `Directory` when `at(D, p)` is an id;
  - prints `Regular File` when `at(D, p)` is a file;
  - prints `Symbolic Link`, `FIFO`, `Socket`, `Character Device`, `Block Device`, `Whiteout`, or any other text, when `at(D, p)` is an other;
  - fails with `No such file or directory` when `at(D, p)` is undefined.
- `create`, `makeFolder`, the order of the s and the reading of s as text: as on Linux.
- `move` and `rename`: the shell commands of Linux, with `mv` in place of `mv -T`.
- The program `rm`, run as `rm -r <disk path>/s`, corresponds to `delete(D, p s)` as on Linux, with the same condition.
- Writing the bytes of `file'` in `<disk path>/s`, then running the program `touch` as `touch -m -d <YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:SSZ> <disk path>/s` with the time of `file'`, corresponds to `write(D, p s, file')` when `stat -f %l <disk path>/s` prints `1`; otherwise the desk refuses `write(D, p s, file')`.

### Windows

- An s corresponds to 1 or more 16 bit units, none of them `0x0000`, `0x002F` (`/`) or `0x005C` (`\`), other than `.` and `..`.
- The desk refuses a `create`, a `makeFolder`, a `move` and a `rename` to an s holding `0x003A` (`:`): NTFS takes `a:b` as a stream of the file `a`.
- `len(s)` is the number of 16 bit units of s.
- `isHidden(s)`: the first unit of s is `0x002E` (`.`).
- `isZip(s)`: the last four units of s are the units of `.zip`.
- `isZipOf(s1, s2)`: s1 is the units of s2 followed by the units of `.zip`.
- `folderNameOk(s)`: s is the units of a 'valid segment' (see Names).
- `fileNameOk(s)`: s is the units of a 'valid file name' (see Names).
- `hiddenNameOk(s)`: the units of s, read as UTF-16, are a 'hidden name' (see Names).
- `okTogether(s1, s2)`: the texts of s1 and s2, read as UTF-16, are not equal after Unicode normalization (NFC) and Unicode simple case folding.
- `timeOk(file)`: as on Linux, where the last modified time is the value `LastWriteTimeUtc` of `Get-Item -LiteralPath <disk path> -Force`.
- `zip(file)`: as on Linux, with each segment as the 16 bit units of its text; undefined also when a segment holds `\`.
- The mount points are the paths that the program `mountvol`, run with no argument, prints for each volume, like `C:\` and `C:\mnt\usb\`, the root `X:\` of each drive whose name is one letter in `Get-PSDrive -PSProvider FileSystem`, also mapped network drives and `subst` drives, and every shared folder of the network, like `\\server\share\`; the letter of a drive is uppercase.
- `<disk path>` starts with `\\?\` (`\\?\UNC\` for a shared folder of the network), so that Windows takes each name as it is; a 'disk path' in the Output is written without that start.
- A 'disk path' given to a program corresponds to a p as on Linux, with `\` in place of `/`, with the command `Get-ChildItem` below in place of `find`, and with `at(D, ...)` an id by the attributes below in place of `stat` printing `directory`.
  For example, with the mount points `C:\` and `C:\mnt\usb\`, the path `C:\mnt\usb\docs\a` corresponds to `id docs a`, with `id` corresponding to `C:\mnt\usb\`.
- The PowerShell command `Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath <disk path> -Force -Name` gives the s of the Es of the folder `at(D, p)`, as strings.
- The PowerShell command `(Get-Item -LiteralPath <disk path> -Force).Attributes` gives the attributes at `<disk path>`. When the attributes hold `ReparsePoint`, the program `fsutil`, run as `fsutil reparsepoint query <disk path>`, prints the tag and the target at `<disk path>`.
  - `at(D, p)` is an other when the tag is `0xA000000C` (a symbolic link), `0xA000001D` (a symbolic link of WSL), `0x8000001B` (an app execution alias), `0x80000023`, `0x80000024`, `0x80000025` or `0x80000026` (a socket, a fifo or a device of WSL), or `0xA0000003` with a target other than `\??\Volume{<guid>}\` (a junction).
  - Otherwise `at(D, p)` is an id when the attributes hold `Directory`, and a file when they do not: a folder where a volume is mounted, a OneDrive placeholder, a compressed file and a deduplicated file are folders and files.
  - `at(D, p)` is undefined when `Get-Item` fails because nothing is at `<disk path>`.
- The PowerShell command `New-Item -Path <disk path>\s -ItemType File` creates the file `<disk path>\s`, and fails when `at(D, p s)` is defined; it corresponds to `create(D, p s, file)` for the file with no content and the current time.
- The PowerShell command `Remove-Item -LiteralPath <disk path>\s -Recurse -Force` corresponds to `delete(D, p s)` when no mount point m has `same(D, m, p s)` or `inside(D, m, p s)`; otherwise the desk refuses `delete(D, p s)`.
- The PowerShell command `New-Item -Path <disk path>\s -ItemType Directory` corresponds to `makeFolder(D, p s)`.
- The PowerShell command `if (Get-Item -LiteralPath <disk path2>\s2 -Force -ErrorAction Ignore) { throw }; Move-Item -LiteralPath <disk path1>\s1 -Destination <disk path2>\s2` corresponds to `move(D, p1 s1, p2 s2)` with the condition of Linux.
- The PowerShell commands `Rename-Item -LiteralPath <disk path>\s -NewName <n>` and then `Rename-Item -LiteralPath <disk path>\<n> -NewName s'`, with `<n>` an s for which `Test-Path -LiteralPath <disk path>\<n>` gives `False`, run when `Test-Path -LiteralPath <disk path>\s'` gives `False` or when s' and s are equal for `-ieq`, correspond to `rename(D, p s, s')`; otherwise the desk refuses `rename(D, p s, s')`.
- The PowerShell command `Set-Content -LiteralPath <disk path>\s -AsByteStream -Force -Value <bytes>`, then `(Get-Item -LiteralPath <disk path>\s -Force).LastWriteTimeUtc= <time>`, with the bytes and the time of `file'`, corresponds to `write(D, p s, file')` when `fsutil hardlink list <disk path>\s` prints one line, or fails because the volume holds no hard links; otherwise the desk refuses `write(D, p s, file')`.
- The order of the s: the first unit that differs decides, and a shorter s comes before the longer s it starts.
- The 'junk file', 'junk folder', 'fixed segment', 'fixed name' and 'clash' rules read s as text: the UTF-16 text of s, where each surrogate without its pair is a character other than `a` to `z`, digits and `_`.

## Notes

Where the code differs from this design, to be adjusted later:
- `delete` and `move` over a mount: the code compares the file system of each folder below the entry with the file system of the entry, so the desk refuses a mount point below the entry, but not a folder below the entry that is the source of a mount elsewhere.
- Windows: the code does not refuse a `write` to a file with more than one name, since Java gives no count of the names of a file there.
- Windows: Java can not reach a name ending with a space or a dot, so every operation on such a name is refused, and Fix ends there.
